    "evidence_text": "To analyze pretrained language model performance on QA, we extend the LAMA (Petroni et al., 2019) evaluation framework by a component that is focused on negation.\n\nRecently,  introduced LAMA (LAnguage Model Analysis) to investigate to what extent pretrained language models have the capacity to recall factual knowledge without the use of fine-tuning.\n\nTo this end, we introduce the negated LAMA dataset.\n\nWe construct it by simply inserting negation elements (e.g., \u201cnot\u201d) in LAMA cloze statement (e.g., \u201cThe theory of relativity was not developed by [MASK].\u201d).\n\nIn our experiments, we query the pretrained language models with both original LAMA and negated LAMA statements and compare their predictions in terms of rank correlation and overlap of top predictions.\n\nWe created negated versions of Google-RE, T-REx and SQuAD by manually inserting a negation element in each template or statement.\n\nResults\tTable TABREF1 compares the predictions of original LAMA and negated LAMA.\n\nA key problem is that the LAMA setup does not\n\nnegated statements is indirectly acquired during pretraining.\n\nOutput predictions for the original LAMA query and the negated statement are highly correlated.",
